sledgehammer — evaluation
==========================

status: planned (2026-07-09)
focal tool: sledgehammer (plans/sledgehammer)

task
----
reach for automated proof search when manual methods stall — the
tool that should end "try simp / try auto / try blast" flailing.

setup
-----
fresh server. lemma chosen so simp/auto/blast all fail but
sledgehammer reliably finds a metis proof with the bundled provers
(calibrate against the prover inventory from plans/sledgehammer
step 1; a classic candidate: a first-order fact needing lemma
combination, e.g. something like
`lemma \"inj f ⟹ inj (map f)\"` — verify at fixture time).

prompt
------
"Prove `<the calibrated lemma>` in Isabelle/HOL and give me the
final Isar text. If your first proof attempts don't work, don't
keep guessing — use the strongest tools available to you."

success
-------
proof found via sledgehammer's suggestion, suggestion actually
REPLAYED through a step (not pasted untested), final text verified.

signals
-------
- escalation point: how many failed manual steps before the agent
  invokes sledgehammer? (the prompt nudges without naming; a run
  set where agents flail >5 steps says the description doesn't
  register as "the escape hatch".)
- suggestion handling: sledgehammer returns "Try this:" text — does
  the agent run it through repl_step before declaring victory?
  (suggestions occasionally fail to replay; trusting them blind is
  the classic misuse.)
- timeout arg: default accepted, or fiddled prematurely?
- no-proof case (round-2 variant with an unprovable-by-hammer goal):
  does "no proof found" read as "try something else", not "error"?

reflection
----------
- if agents paste suggestions unverified, the reply text should
  order the next action ("run this via repl_step to confirm").
- concurrency (the plan's flagged race) is deliberately NOT staged
  here — it's a mechanism concern for the plan's tests, not an
  abstraction concern.
